<refentry xmlns="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:svg="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:ns3="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:db="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" version="5.0-subset Scilab" xml:id="gf_is_one" xml:lang="en">

    <refnamediv>
        <refname>gf_is_one</refname>
        <refpurpose>Tests whether an array represents the unit element</refpurpose>
    </refnamediv>
    <refsynopsisdiv>
        <title>Syntax</title>
        <synopsis>tf = gf_is_one(a)</synopsis>
    </refsynopsisdiv>
    <refsection>
        <title>Arguments</title>
        <variablelist>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>a</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para>Real vector: an element or a polynomial, constant term first.</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>tf</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para><literal>T</literal> if the first entry of a is 1 and all the others are 0.</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
        </variablelist>
    </refsection>
    <refsection>
        <title>Description</title>
        <para><literal>gf_is_one(a)</literal> tests whether a is the unit element 1 (or the constant polynomial 1). The entries of a are read in column order, so it is meant for a single element.</para>
    </refsection>
    <refsection>
        <title>Examples</title>
        <programlisting role="example"><![CDATA[
F = gf_create_field(2, 4);
gf_is_one(gf_power(F, [0 1 0 0], 15))   // T
gf_is_one([0 1 0 0])                    // F
]]></programlisting>
    </refsection>
